---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';
---

<DocShell
   title="API reference"
   summary="The public package API is intentionally narrow. If you are importing a11ied as a library, start from the package entrypoints and ignore the internal file tree."
   links={docsLinks}
>
   <section>
      <h2>Public packages</h2>
      <ul>
         <li><code>@a11ied/contracts</code>: shared runtime schemas and result shapes</li>
         <li><code>@a11ied/core</code>: WCAG lookup, target resolution, driver orchestration, pattern execution, and verification</li>
         <li><code>@a11ied/guidepup</code>: platform adapters for VoiceOver, NVDA, and the virtual reader</li>
         <li><code>@a11ied/storybook</code>: Storybook recipe helpers</li>
         <li><code>@a11ied/wcag-data</code>: pinned standards-data sync and normalization</li>
         <li><code>@a11ied/wcag-engine</code>: lookup, search, coverage, and applicability over generated artifacts</li>
         <li><code>@a11ied/mcp-server</code>: MCP server entrypoint</li>
         <li><code>a11ied</code>: CLI entrypoint and command builder</li>
      </ul>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>What each package owns</h2>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>@a11ied/contracts</code><span>Shared schemas, result types, and command metadata.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>@a11ied/wcag-data</code> and <code>@a11ied/wcag-engine</code><span>The standards brain: pinned artifacts, search, lookup, coverage, and applicability rules.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>@a11ied/guidepup</code> and <code>@a11ied/core</code><span>The execution brain: targets, sessions, patterns, axe runs, verification, and recording support.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>@a11ied/mcp-server</code> and <code>a11ied</code><span>The two operator surfaces: tools over stdio and commands in the terminal.</span></div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2>What this page is for</h2>
      <p>
         This is a map, not a generated API dump. The source-level JSDoc on the public exports is the
         detailed layer. This page tells you which package owns which job so you do not import an
         internal helper just because it happened to be nearby.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note">
      <h2>Use entrypoints, not implementation paths</h2>
      <p>
         If you find yourself importing from deep internal folders, you are probably stepping outside
         the supported surface. Stick to each package's main export unless you are changing the repo
         itself.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
